The second probe lacks the phenolic OH, eliminating efficient phenoxyl-radical formation (B). No diazirine/diazo motif exists in either structure (D eliminated), and Cy5-azide is added after irradiation (E eliminated). Both probes share the identical bicyclo[4.2.0]octadiene core, so any photo-ring-opening/Michael-acceptor release (C) would be common to both; it cannot explain why the light-dependent Cy5 signal difference persists (albeit weaker) after phenol removal.  

The sole remaining light-activated component is therefore the thioxanthenone sensitizer itself, which at 417 nm can still drive low-level protein labeling via direct mechanisms (singlet-O2, H-abstraction, energy transfer to the diene). This accounts for the residual, observable difference on the gel.  

PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A. The residual light-dependent Cy5 signal arises from direct action of the excited thioxanthenone (singlet-O2, H-abstraction, or energy transfer) even without the phenolic moiety.}

